          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Anthony Amendment</strong><br>\nThe 19th Amendment to the U.S. Constitution, which guaranteed women the right to vote, was nicknamed the \"Anthony Amendment\" in recognition of her contributions to its passage, although it was ratified 14 years after her death.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Late Life and Recognition</strong><br>\nIn her later years, Susan B. Anthony received widespread recognition for her lifelong commitment to women's rights. Despite never seeing the amendment ratified, she left an indelible legacy on American society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nSusan B. Anthony died in 1906, without seeing the fulfillment of her work to secure women's suffrage. Her legacy lives on, not only in the successful passage of the 19th Amendment but also in the ongoing fight for gender equality.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
